    "lawCode": "Amd §6-166, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5180A\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to\ncertificates of acceptance\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to preclude identity theft on Oppor-\ntunity to Ballot Petition (OTBP).\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Section 6-166 of the Election Law is amended by adding a new\nsubdivision 3 to read as follows: 3. Individuals nominated to serve on\nthe committee to receive notices, shall, in a certificate signed and\nacknowledged by him or her, and filed as provided in this article,\naccept the nomination of the committee, otherwise such nomination shall\nbe null and void. All certificates of acceptance shall be filed not\nlater than the fourth day after the last day to file petitions for the\nopportunity ballot.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThis portion of this memorandum is not applicable at this time.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation requires that every OTBP submitted to the Board of\nElections be accompanied by a Certificate of Acceptance completed by\nthose appointed on the petition to the 'committee to receive notices'.\nBecause there is no name of a candidate on the OTBP, this additional\nrequirement is essential. New York State law requires candidates to sign\na Certificate of Acceptance to be on the ballot, and the same law should\napply to those appointed to the committee to Receive Notices on an OTBP.\nThese petitions are commonly circulated by political candidates under\nthe ballot line of a political party's endorsement. Individuals selected\nto serve on the 'committees to receive notices' are not required to sign\nany formal acceptance letter stating their intentions, their understand-\ning of the responsibility before them, or that their name on the peti-\ntion is actually calling for a primary.\n\nIt is imperative that those appointed on the OTBP as the 'committee to\nreceive notices' are aware that they hold this position. At this time,\nunbeknownst to them, an individual could be named as part of the\n'committee to receive notices.' Should this happen, and a person discov-\nered that they are on this committee, the only way they could address\nthe submission is to take formal legal action in court, costing thou-\nsands of dollars.\n\nThis legislation addresses this concern by mandating that each member of\nthe Committee to Receive Notices submit, with the OTBP, a signed Certif-\nicate of Acceptance (accepting the position).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
